'''Daniel Paul Ahlers''' (born November 14, 1973) is an American businessman and politician who served in both the [[South Dakota House of Representatives]], from 2006 to 2008 and later from 2017 to 2019, and the [[South Dakota Senate]], from 2008 to 2010. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], Ahler represented the [[South Dakota's 25th legislative district|25th legislative district]], which encompasses [[Dell Rapids, South Dakota|Dell Rapids]] and areas north and northeast of [[Sioux Falls, South Dakota|Sioux Falls]]. 

In [[2020 United States Senate election in South Dakota|2020]], Ahlers was his party's nominee for South Dakota's class 2 [[U.S. Senate]] seat, losing to [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[Mike Rounds]]. Ahlers is his party's nominee for the state's [[2026 South Dakota gubernatorial election|2026 gubernatorial election.]]

== Early life, education, and business career ==
Daniel "Dan" Paul Ahlers was born in [[Dell Rapids, South Dakota]], on November 14, 1973.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=The Voter's Self Defense System|url=http://votesmart.org/|access-date=2020-06-16|website=Vote Smart}}</ref> His family moved to [[Rapid City, South Dakota|Rapid City and]] [[Gillette, Wyoming]], before returning to Dell Rapids when he was in high school.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|date=2008-07-08|title=Cafe thrives in Dell Rapids|url=https://www.pipestonestar.com/articles/cafe-thrives-in-dell-rapids/|access-date=2020-06-16|website=Pipestone County Star}}</ref> He graduated from [[Dell Rapids High School]], where he participated in concert and jazz band, newspaper, basketball, and cross country.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|last=|first=|date=|title=Dan Ahlers|url=https://www.linkedin.com/in/dan-ahlers-26572491/|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=|website=LinkedIn}}</ref> In 1996, Ahlers served as a Senate [[Intern]] to Senator [[Bernie Hunhoff]], Senator Chet Jones, and Senator JoAnn Morford Berg. Ahlers was an assistant manager at [[Menards]] from 1992 to 2001. He graduated from [[Augustana University]] in [[Sioux Falls, South Dakota|Sioux Falls]] in 1997 with a Bachelor's of Arts in government and international affairs.<ref name=":0" />

In 1999, Ahlers opened Video Plus, a video rental store in Dell Rapids. He also opened Video Plus stores in [[Hartford, South Dakota]] and [[Tea, South Dakota]], selling both in 2005.<ref name=":1" /> The Dell Rapids Video Plus closed in 2019.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|first=Patrick|last=Anderson|title=South Dakota movie rental store to close|url=https://www.fredericknewspost.com/public/ap/south-dakota-movie-rental-store-to-close/article_6db4fd1a-c522-5525-8d8a-d214d09d3691.html|access-date=2020-06-16|website=The Frederick News-Post|date=10 March 2019 |language=en}}</ref> Ahlers has been a [[substitute teacher]] in the Dell Rapids School District.<ref name=":3" /> He also owned Jabberwock Coffee House from 2008 to 2011.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":2" />

Ahlers has served community organizations as a Kids Voting South Dakota board member, a Carroll Institute board member, president of the Haven before & after school program, and president of the Dell Rapids Community Fund.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Sneve|first=Joe|title=Meet the candidates: District 25|url=https://www.argusleader.com/story/news/politics/2016/10/28/meet-candidates-district-25/91924880/|access-date=2020-06-16|website=Argus Leader|language=en}}</ref>

In February 2021, Ahlers stepped down as president and interim administrator of the Dell Rapids Chamber of Commerce after 12 years in the role.<ref name=":5">{{Cite web|last=Kaczke|first=Lisa|title=Dan Ahlers, former Democratic legislator from Dell Rapids, announces his candidacy for U.S. Senate|url=https://www.argusleader.com/story/news/politics/2019/09/26/dan-ahlers-democratic-candidate-u-s-senate-south-dakota-legislator-dell-rapids/3773611002/|access-date=2020-06-16|website=Argus Leader|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Media |first=Matt Larson, Big Sioux |date=2021-02-23 |title=City of Dell Rapids Proclaims Dan Ahlers Day of Appreciation |url=https://bigsiouxmedia.com/city-of-dell-rapids-proclaims-dan-ahlers-day-of-appreciation/ |access-date=2023-11-18 |website=Big Sioux Media Sports Network |language=en-US}}</ref>

Ahlers was hired in early 2023 to serve as executive director of the [[South Dakota Democratic Party]]. Ahlers was re-hired in October 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-10-18 |title=South Dakota Democratic Party rehires Dan Ahlers |url=https://www.keloland.com/news/local-news/south-dakota-democratic-party-rehires-dan-ahlers/ |access-date=2023-11-18 |website=KELOLAND.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

== South Dakota State Legislature ==
Ahlers served in the [[South Dakota House of Representatives|state House of Representatives]] in 2007&ndash;08, in the [[South Dakota Senate|state Senate]] in 2009&ndash;10, and again in [[South Dakota House of Representatives|state house]] in 2017&ndash;18. He narrowly lost his 2018 reelection bid, receiving 101 fewer votes than [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Tom Pischke]] to represent the district in 2019&ndash;20.<ref name=":5" />

== U.S. Senate campaign ==
On September 24, 2019, Ahlers filed a Statement of Organization to form a committee to run for the [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] in [[South Dakota]].<ref name=":4">{{cite web|date=September 24, 2019|title=Ahlers for Senate|url=https://www.fec.gov/data/committee/C00720623/|accessdate=September 26, 2019|publisher=fec.gov}}</ref>  Ahlers was the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee for [[United States Senate|U.S. Senate]] in [[South Dakota]] in the [[2020 United States Senate election in South Dakota|2020]] election, which he lost to Republican incumbent [[Mike Rounds]] by 66% to 34%.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Welte |first=Dean |date=2020-11-04 |title=Republican Mike Rounds wins reelection to U.S. Senate from South Dakota |language=en-US |work=KTIV |url=https://ktiv.com/2020/11/03/republican-mike-rounds-wins-reelection-to-u-s-senate-from-south-dakota-2/ |access-date=2020-11-21 |archive-date=2021-09-28 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210928195701/https://ktiv.com/2020/11/03/republican-mike-rounds-wins-reelection-to-u-s-senate-from-south-dakota-2/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

== 2026 South Dakota gubernatorial campaign ==
In February 2026, Ahlers announced his candidacy for the Democratic nomination in the [[2026 South Dakota gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Leader of South Dakota Democratic Party announces his campaign for governor |url=https://southdakotasearchlight.com/briefs/leader-of-south-dakota-democratic-party-announces-his-campaign-for-governor/ |access-date=June 7, 2026 |work=South Dakota Searchlight |date=February 9, 2026}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Ahlers is married to Amy. They have two children.<ref name=":0" />

